public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    /**
     * For any new Case whose Account has Rating = 'Hot', set Priority to 'High'.
     * All other Cases (including those with no Account) are left untouched,
     * keeping whatever Priority they were created with.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: collects all AccountIds first and issues a single query
     * for all related Accounts, instead of querying per Case. This avoids
     * hitting SOQL limits when Cases are inserted in bulk (e.g. up to 200
     * at a time from the ERP integration).
     */
    public static void setPriorityForHotAccounts(List<Case> newCases) {
        // Collect the distinct AccountIds we need to check.
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            // No accounts to look up, nothing to do.
            return;
        }

        // Single bulk query for all the Accounts we care about.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        // Now loop over the Cases and set Priority based on the Account's Rating.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId == null) {
                continue; // No account, keep the Priority as-is.
            }

            Account acc = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
            if (acc != null && acc.Rating == 'Hot') {
                c.Priority = 'High';
            }
            // Otherwise, leave Priority untouched.
        }
    }
}
```

Key design decisions, since the ticket's literal instructions conflict with Salesforce platform constraints:

1. **"One request per new account" cannot be done synchronously from an after-insert trigger.** Callouts are not allowed from trigger context at all (synchronous DML trigger + synchronous HTTP callout together throw `System.CalloutException: You have uncommitted work pending`), and even if that weren't the case, firing up to 200 synchronous callouts in one trigger invocation would blow past governor limits (max 100 callouts per transaction, 10s timeout each) and would also block the record-insert transaction on an external system's latency.

2. To stay bulk-safe and compliant with governor limits while keeping "one request per account" (so the ERP team can trace each account individually, as asked), I queue each new account's callout as its own **asynchronous Queueable** job, chained one after another. Each Queueable makes exactly one callout with a JSON array containing exactly one customer — satisfying "one request per new account" — while the chaining ensures we never exceed the 50-queueable-jobs-per-transaction limit by only enqueueing the *next* job once the current one starts executing (standard chaining pattern), and respects the 100-callout-per-transaction limit (not an issue here since each queueable makes one callout and only chains one more job).

3. The trigger itself does no logic — it delegates to the handler class — and only kicks off the first Queueable in the chain, passing the full list of new Ids. This keeps the trigger thin and the heavy lifting unit-testable in the class.

4. Since callouts are async, "as soon as it is created" means "as soon as possible after commit," which is the best achievable guarantee on this platform without redesigning the ERP contract (e.g., Platform Events or a bulk endpoint) — not in scope per the ticket.

