**Why not literally one `enqueueJob` call per Case inside the trigger loop:** doing so would work fine for small closures but would throw `System.LimitException` as soon as more than 50 cases are closed in one transaction (list view bulk close, or the nightly API batch of 200). Since the task explicitly calls out both bulk list-view closes and a 200-record nightly job as realistic scenarios, enqueuing per-case directly is not production-safe. Batching all closed Case Ids into a single Queueable call, while still treating each Case's Task insert independently via `Database.insert(..., false)`, satisfies both the "async" and "one failure can't affect others" requirements without breaking at scale. If a stricter one-job-per-case design is required (e.g., because the planned vendor integration must isolate API calls per case), the same `CaseSurveyJob` class supports it via the single-Id constructor — the trigger would then need an additional dispatcher (e.g., chaining jobs sequentially, 50 at a time) rather than firing them all synchronously, to stay within limits.

## Notes on the approach

A few things worth flagging before the code:

1. **Calling `Messaging.sendEmail` once per opportunity inside the loop is explicitly against Salesforce bulk best practice.** Each call to `Messaging.sendEmail` consumes a chunk of the governor limits (SOQL/DML-adjacent limits don't directly apply, but there's a limit of 10 `sendEmail` invocations per transaction). With up to 200 opportunities per call, you will hit `System.LimitException: Too many Messaging.sendEmail calls: 11` well before processing the full batch. Bulkifying this (building a `List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>` and calling `Messaging.sendEmail` once with the whole list, or in chunks of 10) is the correct pattern — the per-element failure isolation you want is better achieved by passing `allOrNoneFailures = false` to `sendEmail`, which returns an array of `Messaging.SendEmailResult` so you can tell exactly which messages succeeded/failed without one bad email blocking the rest.
2. Since you've asked for the loop-per-call structure specifically, I've implemented it as requested below, but I **strongly recommend** switching to the bulk pattern. I've included a commented-out bulk-safe alternative in the class so you can see the difference and swap it in — it will fail silently/throw past ~10 opportunities otherwise.
3. I query `Owner.Id` (not just rely on the Id from the input list being the owner) and skip any opportunity whose owner is not reachable (e.g., inactive user, or no owner) — `Messaging.sendEmail` throws for an invalid target user, and since each call is wrapped individually, I catch exceptions per-opportunity so the loop continues and the count stays accurate.
4. Opportunity `CloseDate` is queried along with `Name` and `OwnerId` in a single bulk SOQL (not inside the loop) to avoid SOQL-in-loop issues.
5. I did not add `setTargetObjectId` validation for Contacts/Leads since the owner is always a `User`.

